**What**
The zero-config path from `git init` to a stored value has two dead ends: the
canonical help invocation does not work, and the tools default output in an
empty repository is nothing at all.

**Where**
`crates/git-store/src/main.rs:104-124` (`main`, bare command lists kinds),
`crates/git-store/Cargo.toml` (no man page is built or installed).

**Why it matters**
Reproduced end to end in a fresh repo.

```
$ git init demo && cd demo
$ git store
                          <- nothing. no hint, no next step, exit 0
$ git store --help
No manual entry for git-store
$ git store -h
Store anything in Git as a real tree     <- works, but who types -h?
```

Git intercepts `--help` for external subcommands and execs `man git-<name>`.
The projects headline claim is "the binary is named `git-store`, so gits
external-subcommand dispatch makes `git store ...` work with nothing more than
`PATH`" - and the very first thing a user does after discovering a git
subcommand is ask it for help the git way. That path errors. It will also be
the first thing an audience member tries after the talk.

The empty-repo case is the other half. `git store` printing nothing is
defensible by analogy to `git remote` in a repo with no remotes, but `git
remote` is not the entry point to an unfamiliar system. A new user has no
schema, so *every* command fails until they write one, and the only guidance is
the error text on `put` (`no schema published for kind "recipe"; run (backtick)
git store schema put recipe(backtick)`) - which is a genuinely good error, but
you only reach it if you already guessed a kind name. There is no `git store
init`, no bundled example kind, and the checked-in `crates/git-store/schemas/`
files are only reachable if you cloned the source.

**Options**
- Option A - ship a `git-store.1` man page (generate it from the clap command
  tree with `clap_mangen`, which is a build-script and a `man/` install path)
  and add `--help` handling. Fixes the git-native promise for the cost of one
  build dependency.
- Option B - when there are no kinds, print a two-line hint instead of nothing:
  what a kind is and the one command that creates one. Trade-off: a small
  departure from the silent-when-empty convention, in exchange for the tool
  teaching itself.
- Option C - `git store schema put <kind> --example` or a bundled starter kind,
  so there is a working end-to-end path with no JSON authored by hand. The `-i`
  interactive schema builder already covers most of this and is a nice piece of
  work - it is just not discoverable from a blank prompt.

**Recommendation**
All three; none is more than an afternoon. Option A is the one with real
principle behind it - claiming git-subcommand integration and then failing
`--help` is the kind of small inconsistency a detail-oriented audience notices
and generalizes from. High confidence. Worth checking the sibling projects
(`git anchor`, `git query`, `git forge`) for the same gap, since they will be
shown together and consistency across them is part of the pitch.